#
# Trivy misconfiguration suppressions.
# Reference: https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/
#
# The shared scan workflow discovers this file at the repository root and
# passes it to trivy as --ignorefile (security_scanners/github_actions/trivy.py
# in ROCm/rocm-security-gh). A config-file `ignorefile:` key would NOT work:
# trivy resolves that against its own working directory, which is the
# directory above the scanned checkout.
#
# This is trivy's plain-text format, which takes bare check IDs and so
# disables a check everywhere. Only checks that no manifest in this repository
# could reasonably satisfy belong here. Anything that is wrong for one file but
# worth catching in another stays out, and is handled by `scan.skip-files` in
# .github/scan_tools_configs/trivy.yml instead.
#
# Scoping a suppression to a path, with its justification attached, needs
# trivy's `.trivyignore.yaml` format, which the shared workflow does not look
# for. Tracked in ROCm/rocm-security-gh#37; when that lands, these entries and
# the remaining skip-files both become per-check, per-path entries.

# Every container this repository ships or documents needs a writable root
# filesystem. The ROCm framework images in example/ write JIT and kernel
# caches (MIOpen, Keras, Triton) to it, and the device plugin and labeller
# write the device-plugin socket under /var/lib/kubelet. Enabling it would
# need a writable emptyDir per cache path -- a set that differs per framework
# and per image tag, and that no CI here can validate on GPU hardware.
AVD-KSV-0014

# hostIPC is required for shared-memory transport between ROCm processes.
# docs/user-guide/configuration.md, "Non-Privileged GPU Access", documents it
# as part of a configuration that testing with AMD ROCm containers found to
# give reliable non-privileged GPU access.
AVD-KSV-0008

# The vLLM serving example uses hostNetwork so the model endpoint is reachable
# on the node address, which is what example/vllm-serve/README.md walks
# through.
AVD-KSV-0009
